      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p1_b15",
      "text": "The integration of vision into large language models\n(LLMs) has given rise to Large Vision-Language Models\n(LVLMs) (Liu et al., 2023a; 2024c), marking a pivotal step\nforward in multimodal artificial intelligence. However, this\nsignificant leap is shadowed by a persistent and critical chal-\nlenge: hallucination (Sun et al., 2024; Zhou et al., 2024;\nHuang et al., 2024a; Bai et al., 2024). This failure mode,\ncharacterized by the generation of text that contradicts the\nvisual input, fundamentally compromises the factual ground-\ning of these models. Such unfaithfulness to the visual con-\ntext not only degrades the reliability of LVLMs but also\nposes a significant barrier to their deployment in high-stakes,\nreal-world applications.\n",
      "bbox": [
        307.11,
        462.35,
        543.1,
        615.85
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1228,
        1849,
        2172,
        2463
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 735,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p1_b16",
      "text": "Most studies (Wang et al., 2024; Yang et al., 2025; Yu et al.,\n2024c; Zhang et al., 2024), attribute LVLM hallucinations\nto a dominant language bias, where the model prioritizes\nlinguistic fluency over visual consistency. A key manifesta-\ntion of this bias, as illustrated in the top panel of Figure 2,\nis that the model allocates minimal attention to the image\nwhen generating lengthy responses. This indicates that the\nmodel, in essence, disregards the visual input, relying pre-\n",
